         Time function




           NOW(): indicates the current timestamp in TIMESTAMP format.


           UNIX_TIMESTAMP(date/timestamp): indicates to convert the current time to UTC second.



            FROM_UNIXTIME(number[,format]): indicates to convert the UTC second to the value in TIMESTAMP format.

             format can be set as follows:

               In the Spark, ClickHouse, and MySQL databases, the value of format is in the format of "%Y-%m-%d %H:%i:%s." Connectors (such as hyphens [-] and colons [:]) can be customized. For example, a connector can be a period (.), an underscore (_), or a space. %Y, %m, %d, %H, %i, and %s indicate year, month, date, hour, minute, and second, respectively. The sequence can be adjusted,
                for example, %d_%m_%Y %H-%i-%s.


                  NOTE: 

                  If the value of format is not supported, the value will be transparently transmitted to the target database and will not be converted. The result is returned based on the rules allowed by the target database.





               In the GaussDB V1 and GaussDB 200 databases, the value of format can only be in the format of "%Y-%m-%d %H:%i:%s" or "%H:%i:%s." The return value of %H:%i:%s is of String type.

               In the GaussDB 100 V3R1 database, the value of format can only be in the format of "%Y-%m-%d %H:%i:%s", where %m indicates a full name of a month, for example, 2023-JUNE-08 18:12:16. %Y, %m, %d, %H, %i, and %s indicate year, month, date, hour, minute, and second, respectively. The sequence can be adjusted.



             This function is not supported by PostgreSQL and is parsed as to_timestamp(number).

             The time calculation is converted to the UTC second calculation.





          UNIX_TIMESTAMP:

            This function is not supported by PostgreSQL and SparkSQL and is parsed as EXTRACT(epoch FROM date/timestamp).

            This function is not supported by ClickHouse and is parsed as toUnixTimestamp('date'). There is no modifier on the left of 'date', and milliseconds are not supported.



          TIMESTAMP is not supported by PostgreSQL and is parsed as to_timestamp.

          STR_TO_DATE(str,format): indicates to convert a character string to a date object based on the format character string.

            The value of format is in the format of "%Y-%m-%d."

            The execution results of different databases may be different.

            In the ClickHouse database, when the STR_TO_DATE function is embedded in the input parameter of other time functions, the execution result may be deviated due to the impact of the time zone where the ClickHouse database is located.

            In the GaussDB 100 V3R1 database, str supports only character strings that contain the year, month, and day, for example, '2024-12-01'.



          WEEKDAY(timestamp/date): indicates the Nth day in a week corresponding to a date. In the GaussDB 100 V3R1 database, Sunday to Saturday are represented by 1 to 7. In other databases, Monday to Sunday are represented by 0 to 6.

          DAYOFYEAR(timestamp/date): indicates the Nth day in a year corresponding to a date.

          WEEK(timestamp/date): indicates the Nth week in a year corresponding to a date.

            In the Spark database, Monday is regarded as the first day of a week, and the first week with more than three days is regarded as the first week of a year. The return value ranges from 1 to 53.

            In the ClickHouse database, Sunday is regarded as the first day of a week, and the return value ranges from 0 to 53.



          MAKEDATE(year, dayofyear): returns a date based on a year and the number of a day. year is an integer, indicating the year. dayofyear is an integer, indicating the number of a day in the year.




         SELECT NOW();
SELECT UNIX_TIMESTAMP(TIMESTAMP '2019-07-02 01:02:03.456');
SELECT UNIX_TIMESTAMP(NOW());
SELECT FROM_UNIXTIME(1596094795.123);
SELECT FROM_UNIXTIME(1596094795.123,'%Y-%m-%d %H:%i:%s');
