         String function




           ASCII(str): indicates the ASCII code corresponding to the first character of string.


           CONCAT(str1, str2): indicates the join of two strings.


           LENGTH(str): indicates the number of characters in string.


           LOWER(str): indicates to convert string into lowercase letters.


           LPAD(str, len, fill): indicates to use fill to write a string whose length meets length from left. If the length of string is longer than that required by length, string is truncated to meet the requirement of length.


           LTRIM(str): indicates to delete the blank string at the beginning of string.


           REPLACE(str, from, to): indicates to replace all sub-strings from in string with to, which is case-sensitive.


           RPAD(str, len, fill): indicates to use fill to write a string whose length meets length from right. If the length of string is longer than that required by length, string is truncated to meet the requirement of length.


           RTRIM(str): indicates to delete the blank string at the end of string.


           SUBSTR(str, from, count): indicates to return a sub-string whose length meets the requirement of count from from in the string. If there is no count, return the entire sub-string. By default, truncation is performed by character. In the ClickHouse database, a character string at a specified position is truncated by byte. (Generally, a Chinese character in UTF-8 encoding format occupies three bytes.) To truncate a character string in the ClickHouse database by character, use the substringUTF8 function.


           TRIM(str): indicates to delete the blank string at the beginning and end of string.


           UPPER(str): indicates to convert string into uppercase letters.




