         Time function



          DAYOFMONTH(timestamp/date): indicates the Nth day in a month corresponding to a date.

          DAYOFWEEK(timestamp/date): indicates the Nth day in a week corresponding to a date. This function is the same as WEEKDAY.

          CURDATE(): indicates the current time, which is of the String type and is in the format of "YYYY-MM-DD."

          DATE(timestamp/date): indicates the date of a time, which is of the String type and is in the format of "YYYY-MM-DD."

          DATE_FORMAT(timestamp/date, format): formats a date into the String type based on the value of format. The constraints on format are the same as those on FROM_UNIXTIME.
           In the GaussDB V1 and GaussDB 200 databases, when the value of format is in the format of "%Y-%m-%d %H:%i:%s," the return value contains the time zone. When the input time parameter contains the millisecond value, the return value also contains the millisecond value.

          LOCALTIME(): indicates the current timestamp, which is of the Timestamp format.

          LOCALTIMESTAMP(): indicates the current timestamp, which is of the Timestamp format.

          MAKETIME(hour,minute,second): indicates a time (that is of the String type) calculated based on the values of hour, minute, and second. The value of hour is an integer ranging from 0 to 23, and that of minute and second is an integer ranging from 0 to 59.

          YEAR(timestamp/date): indicates the year corresponding to a date. The time constraints are the same as those on UNIX_TIMESTAMP.

          QUARTER(timestamp/date): indicates the quarter corresponding to a date. The time constraints are the same as those on UNIX_TIMESTAMP.

          MONTH(timestamp/date): indicates the month corresponding to a date. The time constraints are the same as those on UNIX_TIMESTAMP.

          HOUR(timestamp/date): indicates the hour corresponding to a time. The time constraints are the same as those on UNIX_TIMESTAMP.

          MINUTE(timestamp/date): indicates the minute corresponding to a time. The time constraints are the same as those on UNIX_TIMESTAMP.

          TIMESTAMP(date): indicates the time corresponding to a date. The returned data type is the same as that of FROM_UNIXTIME.

          ADDDATE(timestamp/date, INTERVAL x unit): indicates the date arithmetic. The time that a certain interval is added to is returned. x must be an integer and cannot be an expression. The returned data type is the same as that of FROM_UNIXTIME

          DATE_ADD(timestamp/date, INTERVAL x unit): This function is the same as ADDDATE.

          SUBDATE(timestamp/date, INTERVAL x unit): indicates the date arithmetic. The time that a certain interval is subtracted from is returned. x must be an integer and cannot be an expression. The returned data type is the same as that of FROM_UNIXTIME

          DATE_SUB(timestamp/date, INTERVAL x unit): This function is the same as SUBDATE.




